  "slug": "africa-is-a-continent-not-a-country",
  "status": "published",
  "kind": "myth",
  "title": "Africa is a continent, not a country",
  "subtitle": "Fifty-four countries, and the biggest has about 1,900 times more people than the smallest.",
  "countries": [],
  "places": [],
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "claim",
      "title": "The idea",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "It is easy to hear \"Africa\" used as if it were one place, with one government, one language and one story. It is not.",
              "editorial": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "estimate",
          "prompt": "How many countries are there in Africa?",
          "min": 10,
          "max": 100,
          "step": 1,
          "unit": "",
          "answer": 54,
          "decimals": 0,
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "54 in the World Bank's list; the African Union counts 55 members, including the Sahrawi Republic.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-countries",
                "au-members"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "count",
      "title": "Fifty-four countries",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "stat",
          "items": [
            {
              "value": "54",
              "label": "countries in the World Bank's list",
              "cite": [
                "wb-countries"
              ]
            },
            {
              "value": "1.55 billion",
              "label": "people, calculated here from World Bank figures",
              "cite": [
                "wb-population"
              ]
            },
            {
              "value": "30.1 million km²",
              "label": "area, calculated here from World Bank figures",
              "cite": [
                "wb-area"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "The World Bank gives figures for 54 countries in Africa.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-countries"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": " The African Union's own list has 55 members: these 54 and the Sahrawi Republic, which is not one of the 54 here.",
              "cite": [
                "au-members"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": " The United Nations sorts the 54 into five statistical subregions, which it says are for convenience and imply nothing about political ties.",
              "cite": [
                "un-m49"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "Together the 54 hold about 1.55 billion people and about 30.1 million square kilometres, sums calculated here from the World Bank's figures.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-population",
                "wb-area"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "range",
      "title": "How different they are",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "Nigeria has about 237.5 million people. Seychelles has about 122,700.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-population"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": " The largest has roughly 1,900 times more people than the smallest, a ratio calculated here.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-population"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "Algeria covers about 2.38 million square kilometres. Seychelles covers 460.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-area"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": " That is more than 5,000 times as much, calculated here.",
              "cite": [
                "wb-area"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "A country of 123,000 people and one of 237 million cannot be described by a single average, and neither can the continent that holds them.",
              "editorial": true
            }
          ]
        },
